# Kotlin code style

Prefer idiomatic Kotlin. Keep `if` for simple boolean conditions; do not use `if (x != null)` when a scope/std function reads clearer.

## Null handling and scope functions

Prefer `?.let`, safe calls, and Elvis over null `if` checks.

```kotlin
// ❌
if (user != null) {
    save(user)
}

// ✅
user?.let { save(it) }
// ✅ when the lambda only forwards the argument
user?.let(::save)
```

Prefer callable references (`::`) when a scope function or higher-order call only passes the value through:

```kotlin
// ❌
index?.let { addIndex(it) }

// ✅
index?.let(::addIndex)
```

Same idea for `map`, `forEach`, `filter`, etc. when the lambda is a single call with the same parameter.

`takeIf` / `takeUnless` alone in a chain often look odd. Prefer them when they sit in a longer pipeline:

```kotlin
// ❌ awkward as the only step
val x = value.takeIf { it.isValid() }

// ✅ natural in a chain
something
    .takeIf { it.isReady }
    ?.let { transform(it) }
    ?.map { it.id }
```

Use plain `if` for non-null boolean logic (`if (isEnabled)`, `if (count > 0)`).

## Expression bodies

Prefer single-expression functions and omit the explicit return type when the compiler can infer it cleanly:

```kotlin
// ❌
fun label(count: Int): String {
    return "$count items"
}

// ✅
fun label(count: Int) = "$count items"
```

Use a block body when the body needs multiple statements, early returns, or a type that should stay explicit for readability/API clarity.

## Naming: no conditional clauses in names

Name functions for **what they do**, not for the guard or circumstance. Conditions belong in the body (or call site), not in the identifier.

Avoid suffixes/infixes like `IfNeeded`, `IfPossible`, `WhenReady`, `UnlessEmpty`, and similar:

```kotlin
// ❌
fun handleRequestsIfNeeded() { ... }

// ✅
fun handleRequests() { ... }
```

The caller and the implementation already decide *whether* to run; the name should stay a clear verb phrase of the action.

## No local functions

Never declare a function inside another function. If a helper is only used by one function, make it a `private` member (or file-level `private`) and call it:

```kotlin
// ❌
fun process(items: List<Item>) {
    fun format(item: Item) = item.name.uppercase()
    items.forEach { println(format(it)) }
}

// ✅
fun process(items: List<Item>) {
    items.forEach { println(format(it)) }
}

private fun format(item: Item) = item.name.uppercase()
```
